if(pillX < margin.left){pillX = lastX + 6;}
svg.appendChild(el("rect",{x:pillX,y:pillY,width:pillW,height:pillH,fill:"#e8a825",rx:2}));
svg.appendChild(txt("3%", {x:pillX+pillW/2,y:pillY+pillH/2+4,"text-anchor":"middle","font-size":9,"font-weight":700,fill:"#111"}));
svg.appendChild(txt("SINCE Q3 2025", {x:xp(6),y:yp(3)+16,"text-anchor":"middle","font-size":7,"font-weight":700,fill:"#1a3560","font-family":"-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif"}));
if(_cs && _cs.parentNode){ _cs.parentNode.appendChild(svg); }
})();
</script>
</div>
<div style="font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:10px;color:#999;padding:4px 14px 10px;font-style:italic;">Source: Canada Revenue Agency, prescribed interest rates by quarter. &nbsp;|&nbsp; hdq.ca</div>
</div>
</div>
<p style="font-size:11px;color:#666;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;margin-top:6px;">The prescribed rate stepped down from a 6 percent peak in early 2024 to 3 percent by the third quarter of 2025 and has not moved since, the longest stable stretch since the 1 percent era ended in 2022.</p><h2>The Spread That Still Makes the Math Work</h2>

<p>The Government of Canada five year bond yield closed at 3.03 percent on July 6, having eased three basis points from the prior session according to Trading Economics. A prescribed rate loan invested in a diversified fixed income portfolio yielding in that range, or in equities with a higher expected return, still splits meaningfully more income into the lower bracket than a loan made when the prescribed rate sat at 4 percent or 6 percent. The lower the prescribed rate relative to what the borrowed funds can earn, the larger the spread available for splitting.</p>

<p>This strategy operates entirely outside registered accounts. RRSP and TFSA contribution room cannot be transferred between spouses through a prescribed rate loan, and a loan to fund a TFSA or RRSP contribution does not achieve income splitting because those accounts are already tax sheltered or tax deferred on their own terms. The prescribed rate loan strategy is specifically a non-registered account tool, most relevant for household investment income that would otherwise be taxed in the higher earning spouse''s hands, or for funding a minor''s private school or extracurricular costs through a family trust.</p>

<h2>The Deadline That Determines Whether Any of This Works</h2>

<p>The interest on the loan must actually be paid, not accrued or added to the loan balance, by January 30 of the year following the year in which it was charged. For a loan made in 2026, that means interest must be paid on or before January 30, 2027. Missing that deadline in any year causes the attribution rules to apply retroactively to that year and permanently going forward, meaning all future investment income on the loaned funds reverts to being taxed in the lender''s hands. The only remedy at that point is to establish an entirely new loan at whatever the prescribed rate happens to be when the new loan begins, which may no longer be 3 percent.</p>

<h2>The Corporate Rate Moved the Other Way</h2>

<p>The rate for corporate taxpayers'' pertinent loans or indebtedness, which applies to Canadian controlled private corporations with loans to or from non-resident affiliates, rose to 6.3 percent for the third quarter, up from 6.2 percent in the second quarter. This is the first increase in that rate in more than a year and is a distinct calculation from the family income splitting rate. Business owner clients with CCPC structures involving cross border shareholder loans should have this incremental cost flagged before any quarterly loan renewal or restructuring decision.</p>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What They''re Feeling</div>
<p>Clients who have not yet used a prescribed rate loan strategy often assume the opportunity has already passed, since rates were lower in 2020 and 2021. Clients who already have a loan in place may feel reassured by the stability but are not always aware of the January 30 payment deadline, which is the single most common way this strategy fails silently.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div>
<div class="script-box">The CRA just confirmed the prescribed rate stays at 3 percent through September, which is the fifth quarter in a row it has held there. If we have discussed a family loan strategy before and decided to wait, this is a reasonable point to revisit it, because the rate has not moved and Government of Canada yields are still running slightly above it. If you already have a loan in place, I want to confirm the interest was paid by January 30 this year, because that deadline is unforgiving and missing it cancels the benefit permanently.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Who''s Affected</div>
<p><strong>High impact:</strong> Higher income spouses or partners with a lower income family member who could hold non-registered investments, and business owner clients with CCPC structures involving cross border affiliate loans.</p>
<p><strong>Mixed impact:</strong> Families who already have a prescribed rate loan in place at a lower or higher historical rate, where the current rate is not directly relevant but the payment deadline still is.</p>
<p><strong>Potential benefit:</strong> Families with adult children in lower tax brackets or family trusts for minor children, where a new loan established this quarter locks in 3 percent for the duration of the arrangement.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Action Checklist</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Confirm interest was paid, not accrued, on any existing prescribed rate loan by January 30 for the prior year, and flag any loan where this was missed.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Identify clients with a meaningful spread between household marginal tax rates who have not yet explored a prescribed rate loan.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Flag CCPC clients with cross border shareholder loans for a review of the new 6.3 percent corporate PLOI rate before any quarterly renewal.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Calendar a check-in before the fourth quarter 2026 rate announcement in case the rate moves off 3 percent for the first time in over a year.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Follow-Up Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="respond-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> CRA prescribed rate holds at 3 percent<br><br>
Hi [Client Name],<br><br>
The CRA confirmed this week that the prescribed rate used for family income splitting loans stays at 3 percent through September, the fifth quarter in a row at that level.<br><br>
If we have previously discussed a prescribed rate loan for your household, this remains a reasonable window to act, since Government of Canada yields are still running above the prescribed rate. If you already have a loan in place, I want to confirm the annual interest payment deadline of January 30 was met, since missing it cancels the strategy permanently.<br><br>
Let me know if you would like to revisit this.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''respond-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Client Profiles to Target</div>
<p><strong>Dual income households with a significant tax bracket gap:</strong> A DIY investor managing household investments alone may not know a prescribed rate loan exists as a tool, and is likely paying full marginal tax on investment income that could be split.</p>
<p><strong>Business owners with CCPC structures:</strong> Self-directed incorporated professionals often manage shareholder loan compliance without a coordinated review of the quarterly PLOI rate, which just moved for the first time in over a year.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Opening Line</div>
<div class="script-box">The CRA just confirmed the family loan rate holds at 3 percent for a fifth straight quarter. Have you and your household ever looked at whether an income splitting loan makes sense for your investment accounts?</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Value Proposition</div>
<p>A self-directed household paying full marginal tax on non-registered investment income has a specific, quantifiable planning gap: the difference between what they are currently paying and what a properly structured prescribed rate loan would cost them. That gap is easy to calculate and easy to explain, which makes it a natural first conversation with a DIY investor who has never worked with an advisor on tax structuring specifically.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Discovery Questions</div>
<p>Does your household have a meaningful gap between marginal tax rates, and if so, has anyone reviewed whether an income splitting strategy would help?</p>
<p>If you have a family trust or plan to fund a child''s education or activities, do you know whether a prescribed rate loan structure could reduce the tax cost of doing that?</p>
<p>If you run a CCPC, when did anyone last review your shareholder loan structure against the current prescribed and PLOI rates?</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Prospecting Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="prospect-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> The family loan rate just held steady again<br><br>
Hi [Name],<br><br>
The CRA confirmed this week that the prescribed rate used for family income splitting loans stays at 3 percent for a fifth consecutive quarter. If your household has a meaningful gap between marginal tax rates, this is worth ten minutes to review.<br><br>
Would you be open to a short call to see whether this applies to your situation?<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''prospect-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
