<p style="font-size:11px;color:#666;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;margin-top:6px;">Headline CPI has accelerated for three straight months on gasoline prices tied to the Hormuz disruption, even as the Bank''s core measures stayed anchored near target. June''s reading lands five days after the Bank''s decision.</p><h2>Why the Attack Landed at the Worst Possible Moment for a Public Explanation</h2>

<p>Iran''s Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps fired on two commercial vessels transiting the Strait of Hormuz Monday night, according to Axios and CNN, both citing unnamed US officials, hours before the Bank''s blackout takes effect this morning. The timing means the single most relevant new fact for the Bank''s energy price assumptions arrived at almost the last moment the Bank could have addressed it publicly, and now cannot, for the eight days leading into the decision itself.</p>

<p>WTI crude settled at $68.55 on Monday, still down 13 cents on the session and trading near its lowest level in more than four months, a product of OPEC+ agreeing on Sunday to raise output targets by another 188,000 barrels per day starting in August, the fifth consecutive monthly increase. Whether the attack proves to be a genuine reversal of that supply led decline or another short lived spike, as a similar incident in late June was, will now play out entirely without Bank of Canada commentary to help calibrate the market''s read.</p>

<h2>The Widening Gap With the Federal Reserve</h2>

<p>The policy divergence between Ottawa and Washington continues to widen. The Federal Reserve under Chair Kevin Warsh held its own rate in June, but its dot plot showed a majority of participants now expecting at least one increase before year end, a reversal from the cuts the committee had projected as recently as March. The Bank of Canada, by contrast, is priced by markets at roughly a 95 percent probability of another hold on July 15, with essentially no probability assigned to a cut and only a small tail probability of a hike.</p>

<p>The Government of Canada five year bond yield closed at 3.03 percent on July 6, having eased three basis points from the prior session, a level that reflects a market still comfortable with the Bank''s hold path even as the Fed leans the opposite direction. Whether that comfort survives the blackout period intact depends substantially on how the Hormuz situation develops over days the Bank cannot comment on.</p>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What They''re Feeling</div>
<p>Clients focused on their next mortgage renewal or fixed income allocation want a read on July 15 before it happens. The blackout period itself can feel unsettling to clients who are used to central bank officials providing running commentary, especially with a fresh geopolitical headline in play and no Bank voice available to address it.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div>
<div class="script-box">The Bank of Canada goes into its standard pre-decision quiet period today, which happens eight times a year and simply means no officials will comment publicly until the July 15 announcement. Markets are pricing roughly a 95 percent chance of another hold at 2.25 percent. The tanker attack in the Strait of Hormuz overnight is a real development, but it is exactly the kind of headline risk your portfolio''s structure already accounts for, and one week of silence from the Bank does not change the underlying data, which still shows core inflation anchored near 2 percent.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Who''s Affected</div>
<p><strong>High impact:</strong> Clients with mortgages renewing in the next three to six months who are weighing fixed versus variable, since bond yields will move on headlines with no Bank commentary to contextualize them.</p>
<p><strong>Mixed impact:</strong> Fixed income holders watching the GoC five year yield, which could move on Hormuz developments during the blackout window without a Bank statement to anchor expectations.</p>
<p><strong>Potential benefit:</strong> Clients already positioned for a hold through year end, who do not need to react to blackout period volatility since the base case has not changed.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Action Checklist</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Flag clients with mortgage renewals inside the July 7 to July 15 window for a conversation about locking versus waiting for the decision.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Review fixed income duration positioning against the current 95 percent hold pricing, noting the small tail risk priced for a hike.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Prepare a short client note for July 15 itself, since the Bank''s press conference at 10:30 a.m. Eastern will be the first public comment in eight days.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Monitor the Strait of Hormuz story through the blackout window, since it is the most likely source of intraday bond market volatility with no Bank voice to calibrate it.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Follow-Up Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="respond-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> What the Bank of Canada blackout means before July 15<br><br>
Hi [Client Name],<br><br>
The Bank of Canada entered its standard pre-decision quiet period today, ahead of the July 15 rate announcement. This happens eight times a year and simply means no Bank officials will comment publicly until the decision itself.<br><br>
Markets currently price roughly a 95 percent chance of another hold at 2.25 percent. There was also a shipping incident in the Strait of Hormuz overnight, which is a real development but does not change the underlying inflation picture, where core measures remain anchored near 2 percent.<br><br>
I will follow up after the July 15 announcement with anything relevant to your portfolio.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''respond-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Client Profiles to Target</div>
<p><strong>DIY investors with mortgage renewals approaching:</strong> A self-directed investor deciding between fixed and variable during a Bank of Canada blackout period, with a fresh geopolitical headline in play, has no one to call for context on what any of it means for their specific renewal date.</p>
<p><strong>Self-directed fixed income holders:</strong> Investors managing bond ladders or GICs without professional guidance often do not track blackout periods or release calendars, and may misread routine bond market moves during this window as more significant than they are.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Opening Line</div>
<div class="script-box">The Bank of Canada just went quiet for the next eight days ahead of its July 15 decision, right as a shipping incident in the Strait of Hormuz hit the headlines. Do you know how that combination affects your mortgage renewal or bond holdings specifically?</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Value Proposition</div>
<p>A self-directed investor facing a rate decision during a period when the central bank cannot comment publicly, layered with an active geopolitical story, has no professional filter between headlines and portfolio decisions. The value of an advisor in this specific window is not predicting the decision, it is providing the context to avoid overreacting to routine bond market moves that would otherwise look alarming without a Bank statement to explain them.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Discovery Questions</div>
<p>Do you have a mortgage renewal or major borrowing decision landing between now and July 15, and have you thought through both scenarios?</p>
<p>How do you currently decide whether a bond market move is meaningful or just noise, particularly during a period like this when the central bank is not commenting?</p>
<p>Has a geopolitical headline ever changed a financial decision for you before you had time to think it through?</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Prospecting Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="prospect-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> The Bank of Canada just went quiet for eight days<br><br>
Hi [Name],<br><br>
The Bank of Canada entered its pre-decision blackout today ahead of the July 15 rate announcement, the same day a shipping incident in the Strait of Hormuz made headlines. If you have a mortgage renewal or fixed income decision coming up in this window, it is worth ten minutes to walk through what matters and what does not.<br><br>
Would you be open to a short call this week?<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''prospect-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
