</div>
<p style="font-size:11px;color:#666;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;margin-top:6px;">WTI has fallen roughly 25 percent from its late spring range as OPEC+ supply increases and recovering Hormuz traffic outweighed lingering conflict risk in pricing, a trend Monday night''s attack has not yet reversed.</p><h2>Tail Risk Versus Base Case</h2>

<p>The base case, and the more probable outcome, is that this attack follows the pattern set in late June: a violation, international condemnation, and then renewed diplomatic engagement within days rather than a wider escalation. That is exactly what happened after the last comparable incident, when both the United States and Iran sent delegations to Doha within 48 hours of a weekend of attacks across the Persian Gulf.</p>

<p>The tail risk, and the reason this cannot simply be dismissed as noise, is a specific and traceable complication inside Tehran. Iran''s Assembly of Experts publicly instructed its own negotiating team over the weekend to hold firm to red lines set by the new Supreme Leader, Mojtaba Khamenei, son of Ayatollah Ali Khamenei, who was killed in the February 28 strikes that opened the war. The Assembly explicitly ordered that Iran''s nuclear program remain outside the scope of the Doha talks, a demand the United States has previously rejected as a precondition. Indirect talks in Doha last week already ended without progress on the strait''s status before this attack occurred. The multi-day funeral for the elder Khamenei, running through Thursday, has also paused working level contact between the two sides at exactly the moment a fast diplomatic response would matter most.</p>

<h2>What Would Change the Base Case</h2>

<p>The signal to watch is not the attack itself but whether vessel traffic data in the coming days shows shippers actually rerouting away from the strait, which would indicate the market is pricing a genuine supply disruption rather than a contained incident. A second signal is whether the United States responds with strikes on Iranian targets, as officials have suggested is likely; Axios reported Monday night that a US response is expected. A third-party escalation involving Israel is also live: Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu said Tuesday that Israel''s navy would act to protect shipping freedom, a statement made as Iran-backed Houthi forces in Yemen separately renewed threats against Israeli shipping in the Red Sea. None of these signals has resolved as of this writing, and Canadian energy and currency exposure should be assessed against the base case until they do, not against the tail.</p>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What They''re Feeling</div>
<p>Clients following energy headlines closely will be confused that a missile attack barely moved oil prices, and may suspect the news is being underreported or that a bigger move is coming. Clients less engaged with markets may not have seen the story yet and will react more to a client call than to price action. Both groups need the same core fact: the price signal and the news signal are currently pointing in different directions for identifiable reasons.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div>
<div class="script-box">There was a serious incident in the Strait of Hormuz overnight, two commercial vessels were struck, and it is the first real test of the ceasefire arrangement in place since June. Oil prices have barely moved because OPEC+ has been adding supply for months and shipping through the strait had actually been running near record volumes right before this happened. The realistic path from here, based on what happened after a similar incident in late June, is renewed diplomatic talks within days rather than a wider war. I am watching specific signals, including whether the US responds militarily and whether shipping actually reroutes, and I will update you if either of those change the picture.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Who''s Affected</div>
<p><strong>High impact:</strong> Clients with concentrated positions in Canadian energy producers who may expect a price reaction that the market has not delivered, creating a mismatch between the news and their portfolio''s actual movement.</p>
<p><strong>Mixed impact:</strong> Clients holding both energy and gold, where the near term direction of each depends on how the diplomatic signals develop over the coming days.</p>
<p><strong>Potential benefit:</strong> Clients who maintained energy sector exposure through the OPEC+ driven decline and are positioned to benefit if the tail risk scenario develops and prices do eventually reprice higher.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Action Checklist</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Track whether Strait of Hormuz vessel transit counts decline in the coming days as the clearest sign of a genuine supply disruption forming.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Watch for confirmation of a US military response, which officials have suggested is likely, and reassess energy sector positioning if one occurs.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Review client conversations around energy sector concentration to ensure expectations match the current OPEC+ supply dynamic rather than headline volatility.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Note that the Bank of Canada is now in its blackout period ahead of the July 15 decision, so no Bank commentary will help calibrate any of this through next week.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Follow-Up Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="respond-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> The Hormuz attack and why oil barely moved<br><br>
Hi [Client Name],<br><br>
You may have seen reports of a serious incident in the Strait of Hormuz overnight involving two commercial vessels. I wanted to give you the fuller picture rather than have you go on headlines alone.<br><br>
Oil prices have barely reacted, because OPEC+ has been steadily increasing supply for months and shipping through the strait was running near record volumes in the days before this happened. The most likely path from here, based on a similar incident in late June, is renewed diplomatic talks rather than a wider escalation, though there are specific signals I am watching that could change that read.<br><br>
I will follow up if anything meaningful develops.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''respond-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Client Profiles to Target</div>
<p><strong>DIY investors holding Canadian energy stocks:</strong> A self-directed investor watching a serious geopolitical event fail to move prices the way they expected has just discovered a gap between headline risk and market mechanics they may not know how to read.</p>
<p><strong>Investors confused by conflicting signals:</strong> Someone managing their own portfolio through a period where news and prices are telling different stories is a strong candidate for a conversation about how professional analysis handles exactly this kind of disconnect.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Opening Line</div>
<div class="script-box">Did you notice that oil barely moved after last night''s attack on shipping in the Strait of Hormuz? There is a specific reason for that gap between the headline and the price, and it matters for how you think about your energy exposure.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Value Proposition</div>
<p>A self-directed investor reacting to the Hormuz headline without understanding the OPEC+ supply dynamic currently offsetting it risks making a decision based on incomplete information, either panicking into a position or missing one. The value an advisor provides in a moment like this is not calling the outcome, it is holding both the geopolitical risk and the supply mechanics in view at the same time, which is precisely what a self-directed approach struggles to do under a fast moving headline.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Discovery Questions</div>
<p>When you see a headline like last night''s Hormuz attack, what is your process for deciding whether it should change anything in your portfolio?</p>
<p>Do you know what has been offsetting the war risk premium in oil prices over the past several months?</p>
<p>If this situation escalates further, do you know specifically which parts of your portfolio would be affected and how?</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Prospecting Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="prospect-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> Why oil barely moved after last night''s attack<br><br>
Hi [Name],<br><br>
